.TH EMERGE 1 "2026-08-07" "apt-emerge" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
emerge \- Portage-flavoured package manager front-end for Debian
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B emerge
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ action ]
.RI [ package | @set ]
.RI ...
.br
.B emerge
.RB { --sync | --info | --version | --help }
.br
.B dispatch-conf
.br
.B etc-update
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B emerge
speaks Gentoo Portage's command line and prints Portage-style output, but
everything underneath is real Debian tooling \(em apt, dpkg and their indexes.
Nothing is emulated and no state of its own is invented:
.B @selected
really is apt's set of manually-installed packages.
.PP
Two backends are selected automatically. The
.B apt
backend is used when
.BR apt-get (8)
is present: apt resolves, downloads, verifies and installs, and this
translates the experience. The
.B dpkg
backend takes over when apt-get is absent \(em embedded systems that carry
only dpkg \(em and is self-contained, with its own sources parsing, index
sync, dependency resolver, .deb fetch and SHA256 check, gpgv verification and
a real
.I world
file. It is binary-only by design. Force either with
.B --backend
or
.BR EMERGE_BACKEND .
.SH ACTIONS
One action per run. Naming two is refused rather than silently performing
whichever the dispatch order reaches first, which is what used to happen:
.B "emerge -C --depclean foo"
ran the depclean and dropped the removal.
.BR --help ", " --version " and " --info
answer and exit without acting.
.TP
.BR --depclean ", " -c
Remove packages that are not part of the dependency tree of the
.B @world
set. Inspect the list with
.B --pretend
first. On the apt backend this delegates to
.BR "apt-get autoremove" ,
whose auto/manual-install marks are the source of truth; on the dpkg backend
the world file is.
.TP
.B --deselect
Remove the given packages from the
.B @selected
set without unmerging anything. Use it when a package should stay installed
but no longer count as something you asked for, and to clear an entry naming
a package that has gone from the archive \(em
.B --unmerge
cannot, because it skips whatever is not installed. On the apt backend this
marks the package auto-installed; on the dpkg backend it edits the world
file. Combine with
.B --pretend
to see what would change.
.TP
.B --info
Print the versions, paths and settings worth putting in a bug report: the
backend in use, the
.BR dpkg " and " apt-get
versions, the architecture and any foreign ones, the locale, whether
.BR gpgv " and " lzma
are available, how many source entries are enabled, and what the graphical
session looks like from here. Every line is something that has caused a real
misdiagnosis \(em a foreign architecture the dpkg backend cannot represent, a
missing
.B lzma
that changes which index
.B --sync
fetches, an absent
.B gpgv
so nothing verifies the archive, or a locale that stops apt's output being
understood. Answers and exits without touching anything.
.TP
.B --dispatch-conf
Review and merge config files left pending by a merge. See
.B CONFIG FILE MERGING
below.
.TP
.BR --search ", " -s
Search for a case-insensitive regular expression in package names.
.TP
.BR --searchdesc ", " -S
Match the search string against descriptions as well as names.
.TP
.B --sync
Update the package tree. On the apt backend this runs
.BR "apt-get update" .
On the dpkg backend it downloads the Packages indexes named in
.I /etc/apt/sources.list
and
.I /etc/apt/sources.list.d
over http(s):// or file://, verifying each against the archive's signed
Release file. A file:// flat entry pointing at a bare directory of .deb files
\(em a USB stick \(em has an index generated for it on the spot with
.BR dpkg-deb (1).
A deb822 stanza carrying
.B Enabled: no
is skipped, as apt skips it.
.TP
.BR --unmerge ", " -C
Remove the named packages.
.B "WARNING: this action can remove important packages."
Essential and
.B Priority: required
packages are refused. Removals that would take dependents with them are
refused unless
.B --ask
or
.B --pretend
is given.
.IP
What happens then differs by backend, because the underlying tools differ.
.B apt-get remove
takes everything depending on the target with it, so the apt backend shows
that whole list and asks before running it. dpkg refuses to remove a package
another installed one needs, so on the dpkg backend there is no cascade to
show and
.B --ask
does not override anything: it tells you to name the dependents in the same
command, which is what dpkg will accept.
.TP
.BR --version ", " -V
Print two different versions, the active backend, and the copyright and
licence. The first line names the Portage release whose dialect this speaks,
which is a compatibility surface scripts read and is the same in every
release of this program; the second names apt-emerge's own version, which is
the one a bug report needs. See also
.BR --info .
.TP
.BR --help ", " -h
Print the full built-in help, which is more detailed than this page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --ask ", " -a
Show what would be merged, then ask before doing it.
.TP
.BI --backend= apt | dpkg
Force a backend rather than detecting one.
.TP
.BR --buildpkg ", " -b
Build the target from its Debian source package and install the result.
Build dependencies are installed as apt auto-installed packages, so a later
.B --depclean
removes them again. The built version is suffixed
.B +local1
so that
.B @world
upgrades will not silently clobber it.
.B DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS
is honoured and shown as USE flags. Requires deb-src entries and the apt
backend.
.TP
.BR --buildpkgonly ", " -B
Build but do not install. Products are kept in
.IR /var/cache/emerge-dpkg/binpkgs .
.TP
.BR --deep ", " -D
Consider the entire dependency tree rather than only immediate dependencies.
With
.B --update
and a set,
.B "emerge -uD @world"
is the full system upgrade, and on the apt backend it maps to
.BR "apt-get dist-upgrade" .
.TP
.BR --fetchonly ", " -f
Fetch everything the merge would need, but do not install it.
.TP
.B --no-dep-upgrade
Install without
.I upgrading
anything already present. For each target, take the newest version whose
entire dependency closure is already satisfied by what is installed, so the
merge pulls in genuinely new dependencies but never moves an existing one.
This avoids dragging up libc, systemd, the kernel and the reboots they force
just to install a leaf.
.IP
It is not a version pin: it searches older target versions until one fits,
and fails \(em naming the dependency that formed the wall \(em if none does.
When the only obstacle is a same-upstream Debian revision bump, the wall says
so, and flags any session-critical package whose rebuild could restart your
desktop.
.TP
.BI --backtrack= n
How hard to search when a
.B --no-dep-upgrade
resolution is not found first time (default 10; 0 disables the retry). The
first pass takes the first alternative of every
.RB ` "a | b" '
dependency, as apt and dpkg do. If that reports a wall and the closure did
contain a real choice, the search runs again branching on those alternatives
before the wall is believed.
.TP
.BI --with " pkg" [, pkg ...]
Under
.BR --no-dep-upgrade ,
permit these already-installed packages to be upgraded while everything else
still gets the no-upgrade treatment.
.TP
.BR --oneshot ", " -1
Merge as normal but do not add the packages to the world set. On the apt
backend, where
.B @selected
is apt's set of manually-installed packages, the marks apt writes are reset
with
.BR "apt-mark auto" .
Packages already in world stay there: this never removes an existing entry,
it only declines to add a new one.
.TP
.BR --pretend ", " -p
Display what would have been installed. In the printout,
.B N
is new,
.B U
updating,
.B D
downgrading and
.B R
remerging the same version.
.TP
.BR --update ", " -u
Update to the best available version. Without it, targets that are already
installed and current are re-merged, as in Gentoo.
.TP
.BR --verbose ", " -v
Expanded display: per-package download sizes and a total.
.TP
.B --no-verify
Skip signature checking during
.B --sync
on the dpkg backend.
.TP
.BR --newuse ", " -N ", " --quiet ", " -q ", " -t
Accepted for muscle-memory compatibility and ignored.
.PP
An unrecognised long option is an error, not something quietly discarded \(em
.B --no-dep-upgrades
is one letter away from a flag whose whole purpose is safety.
.SH SETS
.TP
.B @world
.B @selected
plus
.BR @system .
.TP
.B @selected
The world file on the dpkg backend; apt's manual-install marks
.RB ( "apt-mark showmanual" )
on the apt backend. Explicitly merged atoms are recorded here; set members
and pulled-in dependencies are not.
.TP
.B @system
Debian's Essential and
.B Priority: required
packages \(em the closest native equivalent of Gentoo's system set.
.PP
The bare words
.B world
and
.B system
are accepted aliases. An unrecognised
.B @name
is an error rather than a package lookup.
.SH ATOMS
An atom is a Debian package name. Portage's category is accepted and
ignored, so
.B app-misc/sl
means
.BR sl ,
Debian having no categories.
.PP
For an exact version use apt's spelling,
.BR sl=5.02-1+b1 ,
rather than Portage's
.BR =sl-5.02-1+b1 ,
which is refused with that advice. Version ranges
.RB ( >= ", " <= )
have no command-line equivalent here;
.B --no-dep-upgrade
is what holds installed versions down.
.PP
A multiarch qualifier such as
.B sl:i386
is Debian's own syntax and is passed through, as is a path to a
.B .deb
file.
.SH CONFIG FILE MERGING
Debian already parks updated config files beside the originals \(em dpkg
writes
.IR file .dpkg-dist,
ucf writes
.IR file .ucf-dist
\(em which is Portage's
.IB ._cfg0000_ file
mechanism under another name. What Debian lacks is a batched review step and
an archive of the previously shipped version, and therefore any hunk-level
merging.
.PP
.B "emerge --dispatch-conf"
archives each package's as-shipped config files at merge time and uses last
time's archive as the common ancestor for a real 3-way merge of your edits
against the new version. Files you never modified, differences that are only
comments or whitespace, and conflict-free merges are applied automatically;
only genuine conflicts are put to you, with keep / replace / merged /
.RB $ EDITOR
/ mergetool choices.
.PP
The first review after installing
.B emerge
has no archived ancestor yet, since the archive is written by installs
.B emerge
itself performed. It looks for one: the version each file was shipped with
is named in
.IR /var/log/dpkg.log ,
and the package is taken from the local apt cache, from an enabled
repository, or from
.B snapshot.debian.org
\(em verified as
.B --sync
verifies an index, against the keyrings your own sources pin, so a
recovered file is trusted no further than the archive keys reach. Set
.I recover-ancestor
to
.B no
to keep a config review off the network. Recovery is native-architecture
only: a package installed solely for a foreign architecture is not found,
and its config files get a two-way review rather than an ancestor taken
from a same-named package of another architecture. A package installed for
two architectures is unaffected, carrying the same version in both.
Requests it makes are bounded:
a shorter socket timeout than
.B --sync
uses, a ceiling on the whole pass, and a check that gives up on the
network once a source accepts a connection without answering it.
.PP
Where no ancestor can be had, the merged and edit choices are still
offered: with no ancestor a change cannot be attributed to either side, so
every difference is marked up as a conflict, but the runs the two files
share are still shared and only the differences are put to you. The external
.I mergetool
is for someone who prefers their own tool, not a requirement for merging.
Reachable as
.B dispatch-conf
and
.BR etc-update .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B emerge -av nano
Install, showing the plan and asking first.
.TP
.B emerge -uD @world
Full system upgrade.
.TP
.B emerge --no-dep-upgrade --with libgbm1 libsdl3-dev
Install libsdl3-dev without upgrading anything installed, except libgbm1,
which the resolver walled on.
.TP
.B emerge -1 strace
Install without recording it in the world set.
.TP
.B emerge -pv @system
Show what re-merging the base system would do.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B EMERGE_BACKEND
Force a backend
.RB ( apt " or " dpkg ),
as
.B --backend
does.
.TP
.B DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS
Passed through to source builds
.RB ( -b " / " -B ),
and displayed as USE flags. Defaults to
.BR "nocheck parallel=<nproc>" .
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Turn coloured output off. Any non-empty value does it, whatever the value
says, so
.B NO_COLOR=0
disables colour as surely as
.BR NO_COLOR=1 :
the convention is that the variable being set is the signal. An
exported-but-empty
.B NO_COLOR
counts as unset. Colour is otherwise used only when standard output is a
terminal.
.TP
.B NOCOLOR
Portage's own spelling, as in
.BR make.conf .
This one is read for its value, not its presence:
.BR true ", " yes " or " 1
turn colour off, and anything else leaves it alone. Note that the two
disagree about the same string --
.B NO_COLOR=0
turns colour off and
.B NOCOLOR=0
does not -- because one is a convention about a variable being set and the
other is a setting.
.TP
.BR EDITOR ", " VISUAL
Editor used by
.BR --dispatch-conf .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/emerge/dispatch-conf.conf
Config-merging settings. Keys: archive-dir, config-protect,
config-protect-mask, frozen-files, automerge, replace-unmodified,
replace-wscomments, recover-ancestor, mergetool, merge, diff.
.I merge
defaults to Portage's own
.BR sdiff (1)
line, so the review's mergetool option works before anyone writes this
file;
.I mergetool
overrides it. Also read from
.I /etc/dispatch-conf.conf
for dispatch-conf compatibility, which
.I /etc/emerge/dispatch-conf.conf
overrides.
.TP
.I /var/lib/emerge/config-archive
As-shipped config files, used as the ancestor for 3-way merges. Written
atomically, so an interrupted run cannot leave a truncated ancestor \(em
which would parse cleanly and misdirect every later merge of that file.
.TP
.I /var/log/dpkg.log
Read to learn which version a config file was shipped with. Its rotations
are read too. A machine with a volatile
.I /var/log
has none, and no ancestor can then be identified;
.B emerge --info
reports which of these are in play.
.TP
.I /var/lib/emerge-dpkg/world
The world file (dpkg backend only).
.TP
.I /var/lib/emerge-dpkg/world.lock
Held while the world file is read and written back, so two
.B emerge
runs cannot lose each other's changes (dpkg backend only). It coordinates
this program with itself and nothing else: it is not dpkg's frontend lock,
and does not make a concurrent
.B dpkg
or
.B apt-get
safe. A run that dies releases it, since the kernel does.
.TP
.I /var/lib/emerge-dpkg/tree
Downloaded Packages indexes (dpkg backend only).
.TP
.I /var/cache/emerge-dpkg/distfiles
Downloaded .deb files (dpkg backend only).
.TP
.I /etc/apt/sources.list ", " /etc/apt/sources.list.d
Repository configuration, in both the one-line and deb822 formats.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on failure, and
.B 130
when interrupted.
.SH BUGS
The dependency search is not a complete SAT solver: alternatives within one
dependency
.RB ( "a | b" )
are taken first-match unless the exhaustive retry is reached, and the search
is bounded by a step budget \(em exceeding it reports a solver limit rather
than claiming no solution exists.
.PP
Session detection is verified on KDE Plasma with sddm; GNOME and the various
greeters are not.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt-get (8),
.BR apt-mark (8),
.BR dpkg (1),
.BR sources.list (5),
.BR dpkg-buildpackage (1)
.PP
.B emerge --help
prints the complete built-in help.
